{"step_index":73,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:38:11Z","thinking":"**Investigating a graph inequality**\n\nConsidering the inequality rad(G) ≤ γ_t(G) for connected graphs, specifically with at least two vertices. Questioning if this holds true. Exploring the implications of high graph radius, aiming to construct a counterexample. High radius suggests long induced paths or structures that have distant centers. This requires further analysis.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating a graph inequality**\n\nAttempting to construct a specific graph to test the inequality $\\gamma_t(G) < rad(G)$, specifically targeting $rad(G) \\ge 4$ and $\\gamma_t(G) \\le 3$. This attempt is halted by the prior derivation showing that if $\\gamma_t(G) \\le 3$, then $rad(G) \\le 2$. The prior proof is now undergoing careful re-verification.\n\nThe proof considers cases based on the total dominating set size, where a size of 2 results in $rad(G) \\le 2$. The total dominating set with size 3 is next to be examined. Every vertex outside the dominating set must be adjacent to a vertex inside the set.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"status","TaskId":"77001943-b7df-4df1-ba5c-db7288bf8e72/task-70","toolAction":"Checking task-70","toolSummary":"Check task-70 status"}}]}
