{"step_index":77,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:38:18Z","thinking":"**Testing the Dominating Set Radius**\n\nConsidering the general case where the total dominating set size is $k$. Trying to determine if it can be proven that for any connected graph, there's a vertex with eccentricity less than or equal to the size of the total dominating set. The question is if the graph's radius is always less than or equal to the set size. Reviewing previously proven cases ($k=2, 3, 4$), as each instance might show a pattern.\n\n\n**Analyzing Radius for Dominating Sets**\n\nConsidering the case where the dominating set has size 5, if the set consists of a single component, then the radius is at most 2. For components of size 3 and 2, a spanning connection can exist between them such that the radius of the graph is less than or equal to 4. Investigating the case of a dominating set of size 6, specifically with three components each of size 2.\nConnectedness implies an auxiliary graph structure, such that the maximum shortest path between components is at most 2.\n\n\n\n**Formalizing Induction on Dominating Sets**\n\nConsidering a dominating set $S$ with connected components $K_i$. Since each $K_i$ has no isolated vertices, $|K_i| \\ge 2$, and $|S| \\ge 2m$ where $m$ is the number of components.\nAll vertices $v \\in V$ belong to some $N(K_i)$. The distance between any two vertices in $U_i = N(K_i)$ is bounded by $2 + diam(K_i)$. Constructing an auxiliary graph $H$ on the components.\nFor any vertex $x \\in U_i$, distance to center $c_i$ in $K_i$, $dist(c_i, x) \\le 1 + rad(K_i)$.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport urllib.request\nimport json\n\nurl = 'https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/search?query=total+domination+number+radius+graph&limit=5&fields=title,abstract'\ntry:\n    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0'})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=5) as response:\n        data = json.loads(response.read().decode())\n        for p in data.get('data', []):\n            print(p.get('title'))\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print('Error:', e)\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Querying Semantic Scholar API","toolSummary":"Search literature via semantic scholar"}}]}
